WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.500
ABBY CAREY: Cloud Code
is a set of extensions

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.300
for the most popular
development tools

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.390
in the market, the
JetBrains family of IDEs,

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:11.750
such as IntelliJ and
Visual Studio Code.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:14.780
In this video, we'll cover the
navigation and capabilities

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.690
of Cloud Code using
the VS Code version.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:19.700
Cloud Code speeds
up your development

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:24.230
by streamlining common workflows
for Python, Go, Node.js,

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.920
and Java applications,
merging non-trivial tasks

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.160
into a simple user
interface right in your IDE.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.890
This means no more switching
between your command line,

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.320
Cloud Console, and code editor.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.840
Tasks, like going through
the loop of building,

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.600
pushing, deploying, and
debugging your application,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.190
can be both burdensome
and error-prone.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.190
Cloud Code handles that for you.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.910
A key goal of Cloud Code is to
enable continuous development

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.080
by automating processes.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.160
For example, you can rapidly
iterate on a live Kubernetes

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.120
cluster or add a Google
Cloud API to your application

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.670
from your IDE.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.340
The first thing you'll notice
when you installed Cloud Code

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:05.840
is the Welcome page.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.900
This provides a summary of
the key supported features

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.320
and also has a direct link to
creating a new project from one

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.840
of our starter templates.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.890
Let's take a look at what Cloud
Code adds to the VS Code UI.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.610
A few icons have been added
to the VS Code activity bar.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.630
Each icon focuses on a separate
task within Cloud Code.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.450
If a certain task doesn't
apply to your workflow,

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.790
you can hide it by
right-clicking it and selecting

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:32.780
Hide.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.450
There's also the addition of
the Cloud Code status bar,

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.770
seen at the bottom of VS Code.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.110
From here, you can
access quick actions,

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.490
like running and creating
a new application.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.060
Cloud Code commands
have also been

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.770
added to the command palette.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:51.610
You can access them by pressing
Command-Shift-P and typing

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:52.280
in Cloud Code.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.910
Let's take a look at the
workflows Cloud Code supports.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.875
First, we have Cloud
Code for Kubernetes.

00:02:05.875 --> 00:02:09.494
I'll create a new Kubernetes
app using one of the templates.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:17.410
Cloud Code lets you run,
debug, and view the logs

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.330
of your Kubernetes apps.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.480
This app is ready to go, so
let's try running and debugging

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.770
it.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:26.840
To deploy this app,
all I need to do

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.910
is click the Cloud
Code status bar

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.420
and select Run on Kubernetes.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.820
Once the deployment is
finished, click the link

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.710
from the log to view
your app running

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.180
on your local
Kubernetes cluster.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.270
To debug your app, view your
code and place a breakpoint.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.510
Go back to the status bar and
select Debug on Kubernetes.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.010
Once you view your app and
trigger the breakpoint,

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.450
VS Code's debugger
activates, allowing

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.700
you to step through your code.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.620
If we head to the Cloud
Code for Kubernetes view,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.290
you'll find the
Kubernetes Explorer.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.380
The Kubernetes Explorer lets
you visualize all the resources

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.130
within your cluster.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:33.210
You can see information like
the IP address of your services

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.520
and the ports they use.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.440
This means that you don't have
to remember all the core CLI

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.520
commands to find this info.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.610
Right-clicking on Resources
provides contextual links

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.700
for actions, such as
viewing the logs from a pod

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.970
to opening a connected terminal,
all from within VS Code.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.290
You can also create a
new cluster from here.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.620
Next, there's Cloud
Code for Cloud Run.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.620
Cloud Run is our fully
managed serverless product

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.590
with auto-scaling
that scales down to 0.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:14.470
As you can see, my Google
Cloud project already

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.140
has a couple Cloud Run
services that are displayed

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.540
in the Cloud Run Explorer.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.750
With Cloud Code, you can
deploy a new containerized app

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.900
to Cloud Run and view the logs
for your Cloud Run services.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:32.970
You can also test
things out locally

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.600
before you deploy by running and
debugging your service on Cloud

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.230
Code's Cloud Run emulator.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.810
Finally, there's Cloud APIs.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.180
All Google Cloud
APIs are listed here.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:52.300
If you select an API, you're
able to quickly enable

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:59.080
it and follow the provided
install instructions.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:03.000
After that, you're ready to
use it with your application.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.610
YAML authoring assistance is
another pillar of Cloud Code.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:09.520
Cloud Code supports a number
of configuration files,

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.180
including Kubernetes resources
like Config Connector and Cloud

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.220
Build configurations.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:19.390
It also supports over 300
custom resource definitions.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:22.450
Here's a quick example of what
YAML authoring assistance looks

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:25.630
like for a Kubernetes
config file.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:29.260
By pressing Control-Space,
I get a variety of snippets

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:30.550
to pick from.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.220
After picking one, I can
tab through the sections

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:33.980
I need to fill in.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:43.140
By pressing Control-Space
again, I get suggestions.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.780
Linting catches syntax
errors, and hovering

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.510
over configurations provides
inline documentation.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.350
In conclusion,
Cloud Code is a tool

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.680
that speeds up non-trivial,
tedious tasks when developing

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.940
Cloud native applications.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.630
It also reduces friction when
creating YAML configuration

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:01.500
files.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.810
To learn more about what
you can do with Cloud Code,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.280
head to this link.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.370
Also check out the description
below for more resources.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.830
We'll go over other
scenarios in future videos.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:12.920
Until next time.

